<LessonShell chapterNumber={3} lessonNumber={6}>

## The Thing You Have Been Doing Since Chapter 2

You have written `int(input(...))` perhaps thirty times by now, and Chapter 2 told you what it does in one line: it turns text into a number. That was enough to get work done.

Now that you know Python has types, conversion can be described properly — and the description explains all the ways it goes wrong.

## Conversion Builds Something New

The first thing to fix is the word. `int(text)` does not *turn* `text` into a number. Nothing about `text` changes; it cannot, because strings are immutable, as Lesson 3.3 showed.

What `int(text)` does is **read** `text` and **build a brand new value** of a different type, which it hands back.

<PythonEnvironment config={newValueConfig} />

Both names are in the panel, holding two different values of two different types. `text` is exactly what it always was.

This matters in practice more than it sounds. A conversion is only useful if you keep what it gives you:

```python
answer = input("How many? ")
int(answer)              # builds a number and throws it away
answer = int(answer)     # builds a number and keeps it
```

The middle line is legal, does its work, and has no effect whatsoever, because nothing was assigned. Python will not warn you.

## `int()` — Two Different Jobs

`int()` behaves differently depending on what you hand it, and both behaviours are worth knowing.

<PythonEnvironment config={intRulesConfig} />

**From a string**, `int()` demands something that looks like a whole number. Surrounding spaces are forgiven — `int("  42  ")` is `42`, which is a real convenience when a person types untidily.

**From a float**, `int()` **chops off** everything after the decimal point. It does not round. `int(7.9)` is `7`, and `int(-7.9)` is `-7` — notice that it truncates *toward zero*, so the negative case is not "rounding down" either.

Chapter 2 mentioned this in passing. It is worth stating plainly, because losing 0.9 of something silently is a real bug:

- **`int()` chops.** Use it to discard a fractional part on purpose.
- **`round()` rounds.** Use it when you want the nearest whole number.

## When Conversion Fails

`int()` is strict about what a whole number looks like, and the failure that surprises everybody is this one:

<PythonEnvironment config={valueErrorConfig} />

`int("3.5")` is a `ValueError`, even though `3.5` is obviously a number. The string does not describe a **whole** number, and `int()` will not quietly discard the `.5` for you — it only chops when given an actual float, never when given text. If you have text like `"3.5"` and want an integer, convert twice: `int(float("3.5"))`, which gives `3`.

The other failures are more predictable, and they all raise `ValueError`:

- `int("hello")` — not a number at all
- `int("")` — somebody pressed Enter without typing
- `int("42 beepers")` — a number with anything else attached

### What You Can Do About It, Today

Lesson 2.4 said guarding against this "needs an `if`, which arrives in Chapter 3". That was half true, and here is the honest full version.

An `if` handles the empty case cleanly, and that is by far the most common one:

```python
answer = input("How many? ")

if answer == "":
  print("You did not type anything")
else:
  count = int(answer)
```

Checking that a string contains *only digits* is also possible with what you have — it takes a loop over the characters, which is Exercise 3. What you cannot yet do is let the conversion fail and recover from it. That is called **exception handling**, it is what `try` and `except` are for, and it is Chapter 11's subject. Chapter 5 also gives you a one-word way to do Exercise 3's digit check.

Until then: check first, convert second.

## `str()` — The One That Never Fails

`str()` takes any value at all and describes it as text. There is no such thing as a value Python cannot print, so there is no such thing as a `str()` that fails.

<PythonEnvironment config={strConfig} />

You met this in Lesson 2.4 as the fix for pressing a number tight against punctuation, and that is still its main job — `"Day " + str(n) + ":"`. Chapter 5's f-strings make it mostly unnecessary, but `str()` remains the thing they are built on.

Note `str(True)` is `"True"`, with a capital T — the text of what you would type in source code.

## `bool()` — What Counts as True

`bool()` converts anything to `True` or `False`, and the rules are worth memorising because Python uses them behind your back.

<PythonEnvironment config={boolConfig} />

The rule is short: **empty and zero are `False`. Everything else is `True`.**

- `0`, `0.0` and `""` are `False`
- every other number is `True`, negative ones included
- every other string is `True`

That last point has a trap in it, and you saw it in the output: **`bool("False")` is `True`.** The string `"False"` is five characters long, so it is not empty, so it is true. If you ask somebody to type `True` or `False` and then call `bool()` on their answer, you get `True` no matter what they typed. Compare the text instead — `answer == "True"`.

### Why This Matters: `if` Does It For You

Python's `if` does not require a boolean. It converts whatever you give it, using exactly these rules. So this works:

```python
name = input("Your name: ")

if name:
  print("Hello,", name)
else:
  print("You did not type anything")
```

`if name:` means "if `name` is not empty". It is idiomatic Python, you will see it constantly, and now you know precisely what it is doing rather than having to take it on faith.

It is also the tidy version of the empty-input check from earlier in this lesson.

